  "activity": {
    "id": "vsepr_activity",
    "name": "VSEPR Theory: Predicting Molecular Geometry",
    "description": "A sequential AR lab guiding students from the minimum-repulsion principle through the five electron-domain geometries, the distinction between electron-domain and molecular geometry, lone pair angular asymmetry, real molecule examples, and two prediction challenges, ending in a recap.",
    "workplace": "vsepr_workplace",
    "start": "step1_intro",
    "actions": [
      {
        "id": "step1_intro",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Introduction to VSEPR Theory",
          "description": "A central atom appears at the center of the workspace. Electron pairs around a central atom repel one another, and the molecule adopts whatever geometry places these electron domains as far apart as possible. Review the flat Lewis structure of water and recall that each single bond, double bond, triple bond, or lone pair counts as one electron domain. Click Start when ready."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "central_atom",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "narration_intro_vsepr",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "intro_reference_area",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "lewis_structure_water_flat",
              "poi": "panel_pos"
            },
            {
              "target": "central_atom",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "label_domain_count_rule",
              "poi": "label_pos",
              "text": "1 domain = 1 bond (single/double/triple) OR 1 lone pair"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "central_atom",
              "type": "*"
            },
            {
              "target": "intro_reference_area",
              "type": "*"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "start"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step2_minimum_repulsion",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "The Minimum-Repulsion Principle",
          "description": "Watch two electron domain clouds drift apart until they settle 180 degrees apart (linear). A third domain is added and the three rearrange into trigonal planar at 120 degrees. A fourth produces a tetrahedron at 109.5 degrees. Faint repulsion vectors appear during each rearrangement and fade once stable. Click Next when you have observed all three configurations."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "electron_domain_set",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "narration_minimum_repulsion",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "electron_domain_set",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "anim_two_domains_repel_linear",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "electron_domain_set",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "anim_three_domains_trigonal_planar",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "electron_domain_set",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "anim_four_domains_tetrahedral",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "electron_domain_set",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "anim_repulsion_vectors",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "electron_domain_set",
              "type": "*"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "next"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step3_geometry_catalog",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Completing the Five Electron-Domain Geometries",
          "description": "Starting from the tetrahedron, a fifth domain is added producing trigonal bipyramidal (3 equatorial at 120\u00b0, 2 axial at 90\u00b0). A sixth domain produces octahedral (all neighbors at 90\u00b0). Rotate the molecule freely to view it from any angle. A summary panel shows all five geometries with their domain counts and characteristic angles. Click Next to continue."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "electron_domain_set",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "narration_geometry_catalog",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "electron_domain_set",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "anim_five_domains_tbp",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "electron_domain_set",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "anim_six_domains_octahedral",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "summary_wall_area",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "image_summary_five_geometries",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "electron_domain_set",
              "type": "*"
            },
            {
              "target": "summary_wall_area",
              "type": "*"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "next"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step4_domain_vs_molecular_geometry",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Electron-Domain Geometry vs. Molecular Geometry",
          "description": "The tetrahedral arrangement returns with four bonding domains representing methane (CH4): electron-domain geometry and molecular geometry both read tetrahedral. One bonded atom is replaced with a lone pair (ammonia, NH3): molecular geometry updates to trigonal pyramidal while electron-domain geometry stays tetrahedral. A second hydrogen is replaced with a lone pair (water, H2O): molecular geometry becomes bent. Notice the electron-domain geometry never changes. Click Next when finished."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "molecule_model_kit",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "narration_domain_vs_molecular_geometry",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "molecule_model_kit",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "anim_ch4_tetrahedral_bonds",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "molecule_model_kit",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "anim_nh3_lone_pair_swap",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "molecule_model_kit",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "anim_h2o_second_lone_pair_swap",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "molecule_model_kit",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "label_electron_domain_geometry",
              "poi": "angle_label_pos",
              "text": "Electron-Domain Geometry: Tetrahedral (constant)"
            },
            {
              "target": "molecule_model_kit",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "label_molecular_geometry",
              "poi": "angle_label_pos",
              "text": "Molecular Geometry: Tetrahedral \u2192 Trigonal Pyramidal \u2192 Bent"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "molecule_model_kit",
              "type": "*"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "next"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step5_lone_pair_asymmetry",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Lone Pair Angular Asymmetry",
          "description": "Methane, ammonia, and water are displayed side by side with bond angles labeled 109.5\u00b0, 107\u00b0, and 104.5\u00b0 respectively. Lone-pair clouds in ammonia and water are rendered larger than bonding domains, showing they occupy more angular space because they are held only by the central atom. Toggle the overlay to compare how the H-X-H angle decreases as lone pairs are added. Click Next to continue."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "molecule_model_kit",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "narration_lone_pair_asymmetry",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "molecule_model_kit",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "label_bond_angle_109_5",
              "poi": "angle_label_pos",
              "text": "CH4: 109.5\u00b0"
            },
            {
              "target": "molecule_model_kit",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "label_bond_angle_107",
              "poi": "angle_label_pos",
              "text": "NH3: 107\u00b0"
            },
            {
              "target": "molecule_model_kit",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "label_bond_angle_104_5",
              "poi": "angle_label_pos",
              "text": "H2O: 104.5\u00b0"
            },
            {
              "target": "molecule_model_kit",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "image_overlay_three_molecules",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "molecule_model_kit",
              "type": "*"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "next"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step6_real_molecule_gallery",
